 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-22",
  "asof": "2026-10-23 09:45 UTC",
  "headline": "The AI trade broke properly this time \u2014 and this book had already taken its money off the table",
  "standfirst": "A two-sigma collapse in one index and a flat print in another: the Nasdaq fell 2.60%, 694 points, and the S&P 1.21% in its worst day since July, with the semiconductor index down more than 6% \u2014 while the Dow rose 0.02%, the Russell 0.11%, six of eleven sectors finished higher and staples gained more than 3%. Meta fell 6.98% to $648.85 after a filing showed its future compute and data-centre commitments far above July's level; Lam Research fell about 9%, CoreWeave 10%, Tesla 3.08%. The bond market took the other side and had its biggest day since August 2025: ten-year \u221218bp to 4.95%, two-year \u221216bp to about 4.65%, thirty-year \u221213bp to 5.29%, with a hike next week down to about 10% and jobless claims at a three-month low of 194,000. France's ten-year rose to 5.06%, a 24-year high, and passed the US ten-year for the first time since 2013. Crude went to a five-month high \u2014 Brent $113.17, up 4%, WTI $99.47 \u2014 after the New York Times reported US and Israeli plans for strikes on Iran after the 3 November midterms; gold fell 2.81% to $4,024.82. In the book three positions are now closed \u2014 OKTA at 202.79 and AAPL at 341.23, both at about +1R, and AMZN at 250.28 on a gap through a breakeven stop \u2014 leaving GOOG, which fell 3.08% to 320.76 and is below water at -0.25R. Q4 realised +34.64 at +1.70R; the day -10.19. Heat has fallen to 1.01R against 3.0R, so 1.99R is unused for the first time in a month.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "today's two events \u00b7 then the Fed and Alphabet",
    "title": "Everything that matters is inside the next seven days",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo retail launch</b> in the US and 70-plus countries, 28 more on the 30th, under a two-per-customer limit \u2014 the position is closed, so this is now a read on the consumer rather than on the book",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FRA",
      "html": "<b>Moody's reviews France</b> at Aa3 negative, with the ten-year at a 24-year high and now above the US \u2014 a one-notch cut takes it to A1",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC \u00b7 GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision and Alphabet Q3 on the same afternoon</b> \u2014 a hike is now only about 10% priced, and Alphabet carries $195-205bn of capex into a market that just de-rated exactly that",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "PCE",
      "html": "<b>September PCE</b> and <b>Amazon Q3</b> \u2014 the inflation print the Fed sees first, and the second hyperscaler to defend its spending",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "VOTE",
      "html": "<b>US midterms</b> \u2014 the date the reported Iran strike plans sit behind, and the deadline the fuel-price politics is working to",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-11-03",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>Today carries two events and neither is in this book any more.</b> The <b>Duo reaches shops</b> in the US and more than seventy countries under a <b>two-unit purchase limit</b>, and <b>Moody's reviews France</b> at <b>Aa3 negative</b> with the OAT at a 24-year high and now yielding more than the Treasury. Both are worth watching as information rather than as exposure: the first is a clean read on whether the consumer will pay $1,999 for a telephone in an energy shock, and the second is the test of whether a formal downgrade does what three sessions of French deterioration could not, which is move an American yield."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>Today carries two events and neither is in this book any more.</b> The <b>Duo reaches shops</b> in the US and more than seventy countries under a <b>two-unit purchase limit</b>, and <b>Moody's reviews France</b> at <b>Aa3 negative</b> with the OAT at a 24-year high and now yielding more than the Treasury. Both are worth watching as information rather than as exposure: the first is a clean read on whether the consumer will pay $1,999 for a telephone in an energy shock, and the second is the test of whether a formal downgrade does what three sessions of French deterioration could not, which is move an American yield.",
